sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: simple task VM slots (2026-11-05 redesign). A slot is a normal task VM, enrolled ahead of time by today's tasks:provision and kept stopped. Claim = start a ready one. Release = destroy it. The pool refills by enrolling a new one. No snapshots, no identity volume, no baked slot units, no new state machine.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · task VM slots (enrol, claim, release, refill)
  %% kind: task_vm.slots
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant VM as Slot VM<br/>normal task VM
  Note over GW,VM: Enrol (pool size per host)
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - a host keeps slots only when its task VM settings name them: `task_vms.slots` = `{count, project_id, size}`, for example `{"count": 5, "project_id": 46, "size": "large"}`; without it, the host has no pool and every provision clones, as today
  %%> - a slot serves one Project and one size: the enrol converge exports that Project's checkout path to the nested network, and the claim needs that size
  %%> - the checkout path is fixed per Project on a task VM (`<apps root>/<project slug>/workspace`, as `tasks:provision` computes it today), so the enrol export and the claim's workspace name the same folder
  %%> - a slot is a task VM row with no group and with `slot_project_id` set
  %%> - a stopped slot that no group holds takes no capacity (`max_vms`), because it holds disk, not memory; an enrolling slot runs, so it takes capacity
  %%> - the pool counts the slots that are ready or enrolling
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), pool size:
  %%> - a stopped slot holds disk (a ZFS clone), not memory, so it takes no capacity, and stopped slots never block a claim or a clone
  %%> - a pool holds at most as many slots as the host can run at once: `max_vms` ÷ the units of the slot size (beast 6 ÷ 3 = 2 large, shark 12 ÷ 3 = 4); a larger `count` is cut to that
  %%> - an enrol runs a VM for a few minutes, so it starts only in capacity that no task, refresh or proof uses, one at a time; a provision that arrives meanwhile waits for capacity as for any VM, at most one enrol long
  GW->>SW: Enrol a slot<br/>today's tasks:provision, no task
  %%> Exactly today's path: clone the current task image, boot, enrol (keys, WireGuard, observer), model key, Pi. Runs in the background whenever the pool is below its size.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - kind `task_vm.slot_enrol`, workflow `operation-task-vm-slot-enrol`: the VM child of tasks:provision, which also attaches a clone of the host's topology seed, then "Converge once" (task VM role phases: nested Incus with the seed pool and the nested network)
  %%> - no workspace, nested fleet, Route or Processes: the claim makes them for the task
  %%> - the slot gets its own Pi token and model key at enrol, as a task VM does today
  %%> - the Gateway records on the slot the digest of what a task image carries in the current release (playbooks, pins, lock files), which the converge applied
  %%> Time, measured on beast 2026-11-06 (op 11948, large slot): 240 s: VM child 136 s, checkout folder 1 s, converge 96 s, stop and check 5 s
  SW->>H: Stop the VM<br/>incus stop
  %%> A stopped VM costs disk, not memory. The Gateway records it as a ready slot.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - the row becomes `parked` with no group, the state that a parked task VM has today; the host sweep leaves slot rows alone
  %%> - a failed enrol is destroyed at once with today's destroy; its Operation keeps the step output
  %%> - the refill of a host enrols again only when the host's last `task_vm.slot_enrol` Operation did not fail in the last 15 minutes; the Operation record is the only state
  Note over GW,VM: Claim (a task starts)
  GW->>SW: Claim a ready slot<br/>task intent
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - `tasks:provision` admission takes the oldest ready slot whose Project and size are the group's and whose image is the host's last promoted image, on a host with capacity for it; a group with `current_recipe` takes only a slot of the current recipe
  %%> - the VM joins the group in the admission transaction; the run gets the receipt of the slot's enrol in place of the VM step's
  %%> - no ready slot: the same admission clones a new VM, exactly as today ("ready slot, else clone" of stage 2)
  %%> - the run is today's `operation-task-provision`: it starts the slot in place of creating a VM
  %%> - "Converge once" runs only when the current release's digest differs from the slot's, so a claim at the same pins skips it and a claim after a playbook change converges, as stage 2 does
  %%> - while the claim runs, the slot stays `parked` with its group; the park observer and the sweep wait for the group lock
  SW->>H: Start the VM<br/>incus start
  %%> Seconds: the VM is already enrolled.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): two pinned host steps, `incus start` and `incus wait … agent`, as in today's resume; the access of Node 209 is unchanged since the enrol, so no SSH converge runs
  %%> Time, measured on beast 2026-11-06 on ready slot tvm-741 with the claim's three commands: 20.4 s and 19.3 s (`incus start` 1.5–1.9 s, agent 17.4–17.8 s, boot 0.4–0.6 s); Swamp adds about 1 s per step, so about 23 s; today's clone path spends about 156 s in its VM step and 57 s in the converge (op 11752)
  SW->>VM: Fetch the task branch<br/>bundle from 209 · task token
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): today's workspace child, unchanged: Node 209 clones with the task token and the VM imports the bundle, then the Project setup steps run. Another lane makes this step fast; the claim measures it on its own, and the claim is correct with or without that change.
  SW->>VM: Reset the nested topology<br/>from the seed's ready copies
  %%> Only for tasks that need the topology.
  %%> The seed clone was attached at enrol; it is the host's seed of the slot's image, because a stage 6 refresh that builds the fleet promotes the image and its seed together and replaces older slots.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a large or medium slot follows the clone rule: while the image's seed holds no ready copies (a refresh with `seed=keep`), no claim takes a slot, and the pool enrols nothing and replaces no stale slot; it still destroys a failed one
  %%> Time, measured on beast 2026-11-06 (op 12083, Project 66, normal slot, no fleet): 89 s from admission to ready, against 334 s median for a clone that day: slot start 17 s, fetch about 10 s (bundle cloned on Node 209, copied, imported), Project setup about 20 s (setup steps 10 s, dependency auth 10 s), Route 19 s; the claim started the refill enrol at once (op 12084, 206 s)
  SW-->>GW: Slot claimed<br/>terminal event
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: today's provision projection; the claimed VM is `ready` and belongs to the group, and the Gateway runs the refill rule.
  Note over GW,VM: Release (the task ends)
  GW->>SW: Destroy the VM<br/>today's task VM destroy
  %%> Exactly today's destroy. The Gateway then enrols a new slot to refill the pool.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): one refill rule per host, run after every enrol, claim, destroy, image promotion and settings change.
  %%> - it does nothing while a slot of the host enrols or is destroyed
  %%> - else it enrols one slot when the pool is below `count`
  %%> - else it destroys one stale slot
  Note over GW,VM: After a release of Orbit
  GW->>SW: Replace ready slots<br/>enrol from the new task image
  %%> Ready slots made from the old image are destroyed and enrolled again, one at a time, so a slot is always ready.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a verified release of Orbit also runs the refill rule on every host with a pool, because a release may change the agent pin.
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - a slot is stale when its image is not the host's last promoted image, or when it was enrolled more than 20 hours ago, the age at which ready copies of its seed expire, or when its enrol installed another agent than the Gateway's current agent pin; a stale slot is never claimed
  %%> - the refill rule destroys one stale slot, and the end of that destroy enrols its replacement
